# std/env, safe reads of environment variables.
#
# The ONLY door to the environment is the raw `env(name)` builtin, and it is
# capability-gated, default-deny: the host must grant each variable by name
# (`--allow-env NAME`, repeatable, or `--allow-all`), and a `.cell` file can
# grant through `allow.env = "A,B"` only when loaded explicitly with --cell.
# A grant refusal surfaces as a CATCHABLE stress (kind `interference`), never
# a crash:
#   env denied, no capability grant covers 'NAME' (grant with --allow-env or --allow-all)
# Catch it with `stress / rescue` when you want to keep running without the
# variable; the wrappers below do exactly that.
#
# What this module adds on top of the builtin (it carries NO grants of its
# own, the sandbox stays exactly as the host configured it):
#   - graceful reads that answer your fallback instead of stressing
#     (env_get, env_get_or), mirroring the std/fs wrapper philosophy;
#   - grant introspection that never leaks the value (env_granted);
#   - honest loud paths: env_fetch (Option value) and env_require (Stress),
#     both of which let a refusal propagate UNTOUCHED. A capability denial
#     is the sandbox speaking, silently reshaping it into null/None would
#     forge evidence that a variable is unset when the read never happened.
#
# GAP (recorded honestly, src/ frozen this batch): there is no enumeration
# builtin. A program can read variables BY NAME only; listing the names
# (the os.environ.keys() shape) does not exist and cannot be built in pure
# .op. Any future env_keys()-style builtin needs a DECISIONS entry first:
# enumeration would leak variable NAMES, so the grant model for it (grant
# the listing? grant per read?) is a policy decision, not a coding one.
# Environment WRITES are equally absent and equally deliberate: the
# environment is read-only to Operon programs.

## The value of an environment variable, or null when it is unset OR the
## read is denied. Never stresses. When the two cases must be told apart,
## call the raw env(name) under stress/rescue yourself.
gene env_get(name) {
    return env_get_or(name, null)
}

## The value of an environment variable, or `fallback` when it is unset OR
## the read is denied. Never stresses, "safe" means "never crashes the
## program", not "silently grants access": denial still denies.
gene env_get_or(name, fallback) {
    stress {
        let v = env(name)
        if v == null {
            return fallback
        }
        return v
    } rescue (e) {
        return fallback
    }
}

## True when a granted read finds the variable SET; false when it is unset
## OR the read is denied (one answer for two causes, documented). Tell the
## causes apart with the raw builtin under stress/rescue.
gene env_has(name) {
    return env_get(name) != null
}

## Grant probe, value-free: true when reading `name` would be permitted
## (whether or not the variable is set), false when the capability gate
## would refuse. The variable's value never crosses this call, use it to
## shape error messages and feature flags without leaking secrets.
gene env_granted(name) {
    stress {
        env(name)
        return true
    } rescue (e) {
        return false
    }
}

## Option-shaped read: `some(value)` when set, `none()` when unset. A grant
## refusal is NOT a value here: the interference stress propagates untouched
## (no containment inside this gene). Pair with `?!` at the call site when
## the caller treats a missing variable as its own early exit.
gene env_fetch(name) {
    let v = env(name)
    if v == null {
        return none()
    }
    return some(v)
}

## Loud read for variables a program cannot proceed without: the value when
## set, a catchable `missing` stress when unset (message says so), and a
## grant refusal propagates as `interference` untouched. The refusal kind is
## preserved so a host can tell "forgot the grant" from "forgot to set it".
gene env_require(name) {
    let v = env(name)
    if v == null {
        raise missing, "env_require: environment variable '" + name + "' is unset (the read itself was granted)"
    }
    return v
}
